# Rules, Skills, and Agent Instruction Manifests

* From Model Capability to Repo Infrastructure

- @Key idea@: An **AI-optimized codebase** is an emergent property of designing a
  codebase for human and agent discoverability, automation, and fast feedback
  - Most of the same properties that shorten human onboarding and make them
    effective, often apply to AI agents

- @Overview@: This lesson walks the development lifecycle
  1. Rules, skills, and instruction manifests
  2. Requirements and planning
  3. Pull request creation, splitting, and review
  4. Documentation as a machine-consumable artifact
  5. Linting, quality gates, and coverage
  6. Unit testing and golden files
  7. Build, release, and CI/CD
  8. Modularization and repository structure
  9. Dockerization and dockerized executables
  10. Production feedback and release monitoring
// TODO(gp): Look into agent-readiness benchmarks (SWE-bench, METR
//   time-horizon evals) to measure whether rules/skills/manifests actually
//   raise agent task success rate

* Why Software Engineering Is Difficult

- @No silver bullet@: Complexity is both essential (the problem itself) vs accidental
  (tools/workarounds)
  - Even if accidental complexity can shrink, essential can't _(Brooks, 1986)_

- @Invisibility@: Unlike physical engineering, software has no natural constraints
  that visibly signal when a design is overbuilt or fragile

- @Combinatorial state explosion@: Every added flag/variable multiplies possible
  system states beyond what humans can reason about

- @Requirements are moving targets@: Building the system changes what people realize
  they actually need

- @Conway's Law@: System structure mirrors the organization's communication
  structure: Human complexity leaks into technical complexity _(Conway, 1968)_

- @Software entropy@: Every patch and extension nudges a system further from anyone's
  full understanding _(code rot)_

- @Brooks's Law@: Adding people to a late project makes it later, due to rising
  coordination overhead _(Brooks, 1975)_
// TODO(gp): Look into tools that manage this complexity: architecture
//   decision records (ADRs), dependency-cruiser/madge for coupling, radon/
//   CodeScene for complexity metrics, Team Topologies for org design

* Lessons Learned in 50 Years of Software Engineering

- @Abstraction & information hiding@: Encapsulate detail behind clean interfaces so
  no one has to hold the whole system in their head _(Parnas, 1972)_

- @Feedback loops beat upfront planning@: Short iterative cycles surface mistakes
  early, cheaper than planning them away _(Agile Manifesto, 2001)_

- @Simplicity is a discipline@: Systems drift toward complexity unless actively
  resisted (e.g., YAGNI, refactoring, deliberate debt paydown)

- @Technical debt is a real tradeoff@: Shortcuts accrue "interest", so teams must
  consciously choose when to pay it down _(Cunningham, 1992)_

- @Design for partial failure@: Distributed systems taught the field to expect
  failure, not avoid it
  - Need retries, circuit breakers, graceful degradation

- @Observability is not optional@: You can't manage complexity you can't see
  - Logging, metrics, and tracing as first-class concerns

- @Shared mental models matter as much as code@: Most fatal complexity comes from
  mismatched understanding between people, not algorithms
// TODO(gp): Look into OpenTelemetry for observability, feature flags
//   (LaunchDarkly) for partial rollout, architecture fitness functions
//   (ArchUnit) to enforce these lessons automatically

* Consistency and Reproducibility Is Key

- @Fact@: One of the few universally agreed rules of software engineering is that
  consistency and conventions speed up development
  - A reader who knows the convention can guess the answer instead of searching for
    it

- @Definition@
  - **Reproducibility** means the same code, config, and data produce the same result
    on any machine, at any time
  - The complement, **consistency**, means the same kind of problem is solved the
    same way everywhere in the codebase

- @Problem@: Without either property, a bug that appears on one machine and not
  another burns hours before the difference is even located
  - _"Works on my machine"_ describes a symptom, not a diagnosis

- @Example@: Site reliability engineering (SRE) treats consistency as a reliability
  property, not a style preference
  - A **runbook** turns a procedure that lives in one engineer's head into exact,
    ordered steps anyone on call can execute under pressure
  - A **checklist** for a deploy or a failover catches the step a tired engineer
    would otherwise skip

- @Key idea@: Rules, skills, and manifests are how a repository encodes consistency
  and reproducibility for a coding agent, the same way a runbook encodes them for an
  on-call engineer
// TODO(gp): Look into reproducibility tools: Nix/Docker for pinned envs, DVC
//   for data, Backstage golden paths, Rundeck/PagerDuty for runbook
//   automation

* Why AI Agents Need Explicit Rules

// From helpers_root/CLAUDE.md
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/coding.rules.md

- @Problem@: A model completes patterns, so with no stated convention it invents
  plausible, but inconsistent, code
  - Writes `dict` where the repo uses `Dict[str, str]` everywhere
  - Reaches for `os.path` where the repo standardizes on `pathlib.Path`
  -

- @Naive Solution@: Restate the conventions in the prompt of every session
  - Cons:
    - Burns context on every run
    - Drifts out of sync with the code, since nothing ties prompt to repo
    - Cannot be reviewed, because it lives in a chat log

- @Solution@: Check the conventions into the repository as versioned files
  - `.ai/rules.md` maps _file type_ $\to$ _rule file_
  - `<TOPIC>.rules.md` holds why and when for principles and decision criteria

- @Key idea@: Rules stored in the repo are reviewed, diffed, and versioned like code,
  so agent behavior changes through a pull request
// TODO(gp): Look into how ruff/mypy already enforce some conventions
//   mechanically instead of by prose

* AGENTS.md as a Machine-Readable Instruction Manifest

// From helpers_root/CLAUDE.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

- @Definition@: An **agent instruction manifest** is a structured description of
  architecture, module boundaries, and canonical commands, written for a program
  rather than for a newcomer

- @Examples@: The same pattern, different names per tool
  - `AGENTS.md`: An open, tool-agnostic standard adopted across coding agents
  - `CLAUDE.md` (Claude Code), `.cursorrules` (Cursor), and GitHub Copilot custom
    instructions are vendor-specific variants of the same idea

- @Components@: What a manifest declares
  - _Architecture_: Components and ownership boundaries
  - _Conventions_: Module naming, import style, test layout
  - _Canonical commands_: The blessed way to test, lint, and enter a container
  - _Pointers_: The rule files that govern each file type

- @Pros@
  - The agent is productive without an interactive onboarding loop
  - Maintainers evolve agent behavior through code review, not a hidden prompt
  - Context cost drops, since the manual is retrieved instead of pasted

- @Remark@: This is not documentation in the traditional sense
  - It is an operating manual whose primary reader is a machine
// TODO(gp): Look into how ruff/mypy already enforce some conventions
//   mechanically instead of by prose
// TODO(ai_gp): Add description of agents.md conventions (there are files)

* Scaling Context Retrieval Beyond a Static Manifest

- @Problem@
  - `AGENTS.md` and `<TOPIC>.rules.md` work while the manifest fits in context
  - A codebase with hundreds of rule files and thousands of source files stops
    fitting in context, no matter how well it is organized
// TODO(ai_gp): Unclear

- @Gap@: Nothing in this stack retrieves the _right_ rule file or the _right_ prior
  implementation on demand, so the agent either loads everything or relies on the
  file paths it happens to already know

- @What it would take@
  - Semantic or embedding-based search over rules, skills, and code
  - E.g., _"how do we name test classes"_

- @Key idea@: A manifest is a whiteboard, not a library; past a certain repo size, an
  agent needs an index, not a longer document
// TODO(gp): Look into embedding-based code/doc search that would close this
//   gap: Sourcegraph Cody context, a Context7-style MCP server, LlamaIndex
//   or txtai for retrieval-augmented rule lookup

* Skill Files: Packaging Repeatable Workflows as Rules

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/skill.rules.md

- @Problem@
  - A rule file describes in natural language what "good" looks like
  - A template is an example of how "good" looks like
  - A skill says how to carry out a recurring task

- @Definition@: A **skill** is a named, versioned procedure stored at
  `skills/<TOPIC>.<ACTION>/SKILL.md`
  - E.g., `testing.write_unit_tests`, `docker.shrink_container`

- @Components@: The sections of a `SKILL.md`
  - _Frontmatter_: `description` and `model`
  - _Goal_: One or two sentences
  - _Workflow_: Numbered, imperative steps
  - _Conventions_: Pointers to relevant `<TOPIC>.rules.md` (never a restatement)
  - _Verification_: How to check the work was done
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Artifact", "Answers", "Example"),
  rows: (
    ("Rule file", "Why and when", "coding.rules.md"),
    ("Skill file", "How, step by step", "coding.rename/SKILL.md"),
    ("Template", "What to copy", "coding.template.py"),
  ),
  caption: "Three artifact types, three questions",
)
```
// TODO(gp): Look into LangChain tools/agents, MCP servers, and prompt/skill
//   registries (LangGraph, Semantic Kernel plugins) as comparable ways to
//   package a repeatable workflow for an agent

* Repo_config.yaml: Machine-Readable Repository Contracts

// TODO(gp): Generalize by looking into other machine-readable repo contracts:
// - Backstage `catalog-info.yaml`
// - Nx `project.json`
// - Bazel `BUILD` files
// - cookiecutter config
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md
// From
// helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.transition_to_repo_config_yml.how_to_guide.md

- @Problem@: An agent asked to build and push an image must answer _"which image
  name?"_ and _"which registry?"_
  - Prose documentation forces search and inference, and both can be wrong

- @Definition@: `repo_config.yaml` is a **machine-readable contract** that encodes
  the operational identity of a runnable directory
  ```yaml
  repo_info:
    repo_name: csfy
  docker_info:
    docker_image_name: csfy
  container_registry_info:
    ghcr: ghcr.io/causify-ai
  runnable_dir_info:
    use_helpers_as_nested_module: 1
    venv_tag: helpers
  ```

- @Remark@: The file replaced an executable `repo_config.py`
  - Config is now _data_, read through `hrecouti.get_repo_config()` or `hserver`
  - `IS_SUPER_REPO` was renamed `USE_HELPERS_AS_NESTED_MODULE` for clarity

- @Key idea@: A release task derives the registry and the naming convention from the
  contract, instead of relying on environment-specific assumptions

# Requirements and Planning

* Automating Requirements Capture

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/specs.write

- @Problem@: An agent handed a one-line issue title fills the gap between _"what was
  asked"_ and _"what to build"_ with assumptions
  - A wrong assumption compounds every commit downstream

- @Definition@: A **specification** is the artifact that separates "what does the
  user want" from "how is it implemented", written before a branch exists

- @Solution@: The `specs.write` skill drafts a specification from a request, covering
  scope, interface, edge cases, and out-of-scope items explicitly

- @Constraints@: Keep the spec from drift by:
  - Stopping for human approval before implementation starts
  - Referencing the spec file from the pull request, so reviewers check code against
    a written contract, not against the issue title

- @Key idea@: The earliest and cheapest place to catch a misunderstanding is before
  the first line of code, not in review

* Architecture Decision Records

- @Problem@: A repository's rules and manifests capture the current convention, but
  not why an earlier alternative was rejected
  - An agent explores a settled tradeoff every time it is asked to reconsider one

- @Definition@: An **architecture decision record (ADR)** is a short, dated,
  versioned file recording one decision:
  - The context
  - The options considered
  - The choice and its consequences

- @Solution@: Decisions need to be stored as structured, queryable artifacts
  - The reasoning should not live in pull request discussions and chat threads an
    agent cannot cheaply retrieve
  - A `docs/adr/NNNN-<slug>.md` convention with a fixed template
  - A skill that drafts / updates an ADR from a discussion and links it from the
    affected rule file

- @Key idea@
  - A rule file says what to do today
  - An ADR says why today's rule beat other rules

# Code Modularization and Repo Structure

* Single vs Multiple Repo

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.integrate_repos.how_to_guide.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 2

- @Problem@: Teams want the _modularity_ of many repositories and the _environment
  consistency_ of one, and the classic answer sacrifices one
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Approach", "Pros", "Cons"),
  rows: (
    ("Monorepo",
     "One commit history, no version skew, shared tooling",
     "Long CI builds, extra build tools, weak boundaries, hard permissions"),
    ("Multi-repo",
     "Independent lifecycles, small checkouts, clear ownership",
     "Dependency skew across repos, costly change propagation"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.7fr, 1.3fr, 1.5fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: The real question:
  - Is not _"one repo or many"_
  - Is _"what is the smallest unit that can be built, tested, and released on its
    own"_
// TODO(gp): Look into monorepo tooling (Bazel, Nx, Turborepo, Lerna) and
//   multi-repo tooling (git submodules/subtrees, Meta's Sapling) for how
//   other stacks solve the same tradeoff

* Runnable Directories: A Hybrid Architecture

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.runnable_repo.reference.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Parts 2 and 4

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Definition@: A directory is **runnable** when it carries its container and
  dependencies, so that it can be deployed as a unit

- @Components@: What a runnable directory contains
  - `devops`: Build, run, and release the container
  - `dev_scripts_<name>`: Thin environment and `setenv`
  - `repo_config.yaml`: The machine-readable contract
  - Common files symlinked back to `//helpers`

::::
:::: {.column width=46%}

```graphviz
digraph RunnableRepo {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.26;
  ranksep=0.40;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=11,
        margin="0.18,0.10",
        height=0.44];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.7,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=9,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  // repo : blue | runnable dir : sage green | shared toolchain : violet

  Repo [label="Runnable repo",
        fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  Dir1 [label="runnable dir 1\ndevops + tasks.py",
        fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Dir2 [label="runnable dir 2\ndevops + tasks.py",
        fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Help [label="//helpers\nsubmodule",
        fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];

  Repo -> Dir1;
  Repo -> Dir2;
  Repo -> Help [label="  toolchain", style=dashed];
  Dir1 -> Help [style=dashed];
  Dir2 -> Help [style=dashed];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Goals@: Design targets the architecture is aimed at:
  - Dependencies pinned by a Python package manager (`poetry`, `uv`, Conda), not by
    hand
  - Devops builds and releases the container; developers only use it
  - One image builds for both Intel and Arm, on Linux, macOS, and WSL

- @Key idea@: A runnable directory is a **context boundary**, so the agent's prompt,
  container, and test scope coincide, which cuts cross-cutting edits and context
  hallucinations
// TODO(gp): Look into similar boundary mechanisms: Bazel `BUILD` target
//   sandboxing, Nx module-boundary lint rules, the Pants build system,
//   per-package Dev Containers

* Package Structure and Import Conventions

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.imports_and_packages.how_to_guide.md
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.import_check.reference.md

- @Goal@: Python packages simplify client imports, hide which file holds the code,
  group related units, and make import loops preventable

- @Algorithm@: The import rules
  - Inside a package, import sibling _files_ directly
  - From outside, import the _package_ (so internals can be reorganized freely)

- @Example@: The rule is machine-checkable
  - Detect import cycles
  - Render the dependency graph, including a cycles-only view
  - Automate abbreviations of the package for visibility and consistency
    - E.g., `import datapull.XYZ.data.client as itdcl`

- @Key idea@: An explicit dependency order is a boundary an agent respects and
  continuous integration verifies

* Automating Code Modularization with AI

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/coding.factor_common_code

- @Problem@: Generated code duplicates helper logic across files, because each
  generation starts from the local context only

- @Solution@: The `coding.factor_common_code` skill
  - Detects exact and structurally similar blocks across the given files
  - Proposes a function interface: Name, parameters, return value
  - Reports duplicate locations
  - Applies the refactoring after the proposal is accepted

- @Constraints@: What keeps the refactoring honest
  - Behavior must not change
  - Skip trivial extractions of fewer than two or three meaningful lines
  - Prefer logic with one clear responsibility that will change in one place

- @Key idea@: Deduplication is easy to detect and easy to overdo, so the tool
  proposes and a human decides
// TODO(gp): Look into jscpd, PMD CPD, and SonarQube's duplication detector as
//   language-agnostic alternatives, and Sourcegraph batch changes for
//   applying the refactor at scale

* Automating Cross-Repo Renames and Reference Updates

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/git.move
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/git.update_references
// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/coding.rename

- @Problem@
  - Renaming a function or moving a file means finding every reference across
    repositories a human would search by hand
  - Change propagation is costly in multi-repo approach

- @Solution@: Skills to cover one move end to end
  - `git.move` relocates a file or directory and rewrites references inside its own
    repository
  - `git.update_references` finds and updates references to that path in _other_ Git
    repositories that depend on it
  - `coding.rename` performs the same propagation for a function or variable name,
    not just a path
  - After find-and-replace across text, every reference is a match found by static
    analysis

- @Key idea@: The propagation cost that makes multi-repo expensive for a human is
  exactly the mechanical, rule-following work an agent automates cheaply
// TODO(gp): Look into codemod frameworks that do the same job outside this
//   stack: jscodeshift, Comby, OpenRewrite, and LSP-based rename (rope,
//   ts-morph)

# Dockerization and Dockerized Executables

* Thin Environment and Containerized Development

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/thin_environment/all.thin_environment.reference.md

- @Definition@: The **thin environment** approach is a Python virtual environment
  holding the minimum packages needed to start the development system in Docker
// TODO(gp): too much detail, point to the documentation

- @Components@: The files under `dev_scripts_<repo>/thin_client/`
  - `build.py` and `requirements.txt`: Create the environment
  - `setenv.sh`: Configure a shell, inside or outside the container
  - `tmux.py`: Build the standard multi-window session
  - `thin_client_utils.sh` and `thin_client_utils.py`: Shared code

- @Example@: Bootstrap in four commands
  ```bash
  > dev_scripts_helpers/thin_client/build.py
  > source dev_scripts_helpers/thin_client/setenv.sh
  > i docker_build_local_image --version 1.0.0
  > i docker_bash --skip-pull
  ```

- @Pros@: The host keeps only Docker and a handful of packages
  - The laptop, the server, and the CI runner behave identically
  - Installing the Git hooks and configuring the environment are a side effect of
    activating the environment

- @Key idea@: An agent can be dropped into any machine and reach the same environment
  through the same two commands
// TODO(gp): Look into the VS Code Dev Containers spec, Nix flakes + direnv,
//   and Gitpod/GitHub Codespaces as alternative reproducible-dev-env
//   approaches

* Dockerized Executables

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/docker/all.docker.tutorial.md
// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 3

- @Problem@: Tools installed on the host drift per platform, and folding every tool
  into one development image produces a bloated, fragile container

- @Definition@: A **dockerized executable** is a slim purpose-built image plus a thin
  wrapper script, so that calling it looks like calling a local program

- @Components@: What the wrapper is responsible for
  - _Repository discovery_: Find the repo root and mount it at `/src`
  - _Path translation_: Rewrite the paths from host to docker space (e.g.,
    `docs/file.md` to `/src/docs/file.md`)
  - _Image lifecycle_: Pull or build when needed, then remove the container
  - _Permissions_: Run as the caller's `UID:GID` so outputs are owned correctly
  - _Isolation_: Forward only whitelisted environment variables
  - _Output_: Stream stdout and stderr, and propagate the exit code

- @Example@: The call site shows nothing about containers
  ```bash
  > convert_docx_to_markdown.py -i docs/update.docx -o docs/update.md
  ```

- @Remark@: The pattern is not tied to one tool
  - E.g., running non-python packages (e.g., `prettier` for formatting,
    Mermaid/Graphviz/TikZ diagram rendering, LaTeX)
  - They are an alternative to `uvx`

- @Cons@
  - Docker becomes a hard dependency
  - Images consume disk
  - The first run pays the price of a Docker pull or build
// TODO(gp): Look into Homebrew formulae that wrap a container, `act` for
//   running GitHub Actions locally, and `just`/`entr` as other thin-wrapper
//   patterns

* Children vs Sibling Containers

// From
// helpers_root/docs/tools/docker/all.dind_and_sibling_containers.how_to_guide.md

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Too much details, point to blog and documentation

- @Comparison@: Two ways to launch a container from inside one
  - _Children containers_ (aka Docker-in-Docker): The outer container runs its own
    daemon
    - Cons: Needs `--privileged`, which is often refused
    - Cons: The build cache is not shared, so images are rebuilt after every restart
  - _Sibling containers_ : Mount `/var/run/docker.sock` and talk to the host daemon
    - Pros: No privilege escalation, and the build cache is shared
    - Cons: Less isolation, and spawned containers can be left hanging
    - Cons: Mounts must name host-visible paths, so the wrapper translates them

::::
:::: {.column width=46%}

```graphviz
digraph Containers {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.26;
  ranksep=0.40;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=11,
        margin="0.18,0.10",
        height=0.44];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.7,
