//- @Variants@: The prompt selects the lens
//  - `review_correctness`, `review_architecture`, `review_linter`, `review_llm`

- @Key idea@: The review criteria live in the same style-guide file the linter reads,
  so tool and reviewer share one source of truth
// TODO(gp): Look into reviewdog for turning linter output into inline PR
//   comments, and Danger/danger.js for policy-as-code review rules

* Automating PR Review with AI (2/2)

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/all.code_review_tools.reference.md
// From helpers_root/docs/code_guidelines/all.code_review.how_to_guide.md

- @Examples@: Hosted review systems
  - _GitHub Copilot Code Review_: In-platform, minimal setup, shallow analysis
  - _Graphite_: Pull request workflow and stacked diffs, light on semantics
  - _CodeRabbit_: Deeper logic and bug analysis, younger integrations
  - _Snyk Code_: Static analysis aimed at security and anti-patterns

- @Limitations@
  - Suggestions are best guesses and need human oversight
  - Project-specific context is exactly what a hosted tool lacks
  - Pricing is per seat per month, so cost scales linearly with team size

- @Fact@: The human checklist is not automated away
  - Is the design right, and is the code more complex than it needs to be?
  - Are the tests well designed, and do comments explain _why_ not _what_?
  - Is the developer building something they do not need yet?

- @Key idea@: Automate the mechanical pass, and spend the human pass on architecture,
  specification conformity, and intent
// TODO(gp): Look into Codacy, DeepSource, Qodo (CodiumAI), Greptile, and the
//   open-source PR-Agent as further hosted or self-hosted reviewers

* The Agentic Loop: Plan, Patch, Prove, Summarize

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Definition@: The **agentic loop** is the disciplined workflow the infrastructure
  makes possible
  - _Plan_: State the intended change and the affected runnable directory
  - _Patch_: Implement a minimal diff that respects directory boundaries
  - _Prove_: Run the cheapest gate first, then widen
  - _Summarize_: Produce a pull-request-ready explanation with evidence
// - @Example@: The escalation order inside _Prove_
//   ```bash
//   > invoke lint --modified
//   > invoke run_fast_tests
//   > invoke run_slow_tests
//   ```

- @Key idea@: The novelty is not that an agent writes code, it is that the change
  arrives carrying its own evidence

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph AgenticLoop {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.28;
  ranksep=0.42;
  rankdir=TB;

  node [shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#FFFFFF",
        color="#9AA9B8",
        penwidth=1.8,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontcolor="#243B53",
        fontsize=12,
        margin="0.20,0.12",
        height=0.46];

  edge [color="#A3B1C0",
        penwidth=1.3,
        arrowhead=vee,
        arrowsize=0.75,
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=10,
        fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  // plan : teal | patch : blue | prove : sage green | summary : orange

  Plan  [label="Plan",
         fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
  Patch [label="Patch",
         fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  Prove [label="Prove\n(lint, tests)",
         fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
  Sum   [label="Summarize",
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];

  Plan -> Patch;
  Patch -> Prove;
  Prove -> Sum [label="  Gates green"];
  Prove -> Patch [label="  Gate fails",
                  style=dashed, penwidth=1.6,
                  color="#C0455B", fontcolor="#C0455B",
                  constraint=false];
}
```

