* Automating Root-Cause Analysis When a Gate Fails

- @Problem@: The agentic loop's dashed edge _"Gate fails $\to$ back to Patch"_ hides
  the hardest step
  - Knowing a test failed is not the same as knowing why, and an agent that patches
    without a cause thrashes

- @Gap@: Nothing in this stack automates the diagnosis itself
  - No bisection: Which commit in the branch introduced the failure?
  - No log or stack-trace clustering: Is this failure the same one from three pull
    requests ago, or new

- @What it would take@
  - `git bisect` driven by the agent, running the failing test at each step
  - A skill that groups CI failures by stack-trace similarity, so a recurring flake
    is recognized instead of re-diagnosed from zero
// TODO(gp): Look into automated `git bisect` runners, Sentry/Datadog error
//   clustering by stack-trace fingerprint, and BuildPulse for grouping
//   recurring CI failures

# Automating Documentation

* Documentation as a Machine-Consumable Artifact

// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.diataxis.explanation.md
// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md

- @Problem@: An essay-shaped document is expensive for a human to skim and expensive
  for an AI agent to search

- @Definition@: **Diataxis** splits documentation into four modes, each with one
  clear job
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Mode", "Orientation", "Reader question", "Suffix"),
  rows: (
    ("Tutorial", "Learning", "Teach me by doing", ".tutorial.md"),
    ("How-to guide", "Goal", "Help me do this task", ".how_to_guide.md"),
    ("Reference", "Information", "Tell me the details", ".reference.md"),
    ("Explanation", "Understanding", "Explain the design", ".explanation.md"),
  ),
)
```

- File name encodes the mode: `docs/{component}/{audience}.{topic}.{diataxis_tag}.md`

- @Techniques@: Conventions that make a document machine-consumable
  - A summary in bullets for incremental disclosure
  - Meaningful headers and clear conventions (e.g., one idea per bullet, DRY applied
    to documentation)
  - A loop that keeps the content up to date (e.g., ensure the content is in sync
    with the code, check referred URLs, check markdown links...)
  - Machine readable and generated diagrams

- @Key idea@: The structure that makes a document skimmable for a human is the same
  structure that makes it easily searchable and consumable for an AI assistant
// TODO(ai_gp): Prefer markdown
// TODO(gp): Look into doc tooling: Docusaurus/MkDocs renderers, Vale/alex for
//   prose linting, and RAG doc-indexing tools (LlamaIndex, Context7) for
//   agent retrieval

* Generating and Maintaining READMEs with AI

// From helpers_root/.claude/skills/readme.create
// From helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.markdown_tools.explanation.md

- @Problem@: A `README` is written once and rots, because nothing fails when it goes
  stale
  - Different types of README (e.g., for a dir, an executable, a Python library)
  - Summary, structure of the directory, description of files
  - Description of executables, workflows, and architecture when they apply

- @Key idea@: Generation is cheap
  - Check that the document still matches the code
  - Check referred URLs
  - Check relationships with other markdown links
  - Keep it updated (incrementally when something change + a top to bottom run once
    in a while)
// TODO(ai_gp): Systems to keep documentation updated
// ctx7?
// TODO(gp): Look into readme-ai, Mintlify writer, and link checkers
//   (markdown-link-check, lychee) for automated README generation and upkeep

# Automating Code Quality

* Pre-commit Tool

// TODO(gp): Look into the pre-commit framework (pre-commit.com) and how it
//   compares to this repo's custom `pre-commit.py` hook runner

* Pre-Commit Hooks

// From helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git_hooks.reference.md
// From helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git_hooks.how_to_guide.md

::: columns
:::: {.column width=54%}

- @Definition@: A **git hook** is a script Git runs around an event such as a commit

- @Examples@ of checks
  - Check commit message conventions
  - Never commit on the default branch (e.g., `master`) but always use a PR
  - Name and email for the author are configured
  - Refuse oversized blobs
  - Make sure all the source code (e.g., Python) at least parses
  - No secret in the staged diff
  - Check leftover merge-conflict markers
  - Lint was run and passed
  - Unit tests are present and have coverage of the new code

- @Key idea@: Gates are ordered by cost, so the cheapest check runs first

::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
