::::
:::

- @Key idea@: Git versions the code and `changelog.txt` versions the container, so a
  commit always names the environment that can run it
// TODO(gp): Look into Skopeo for registry-to-registry image copy, cosign for
//   image signing, and Syft/Grype for an SBOM plus vulnerability check at
//   each stage

# Production Feedback and Release Monitoring

* Closing the Loop: Feeding Production Incidents Back to the Agent

- @Problem@: The lifecycle in this lesson ends at "Merge" and "push to registry";
  nothing routes a production error back to a filed issue, so the feedback loop that
  catches a mistake before merge does not exist after merge

- @Gap@: An exception in production and a failing assertion in CI are the same
  signal, "the code did something wrong", but only the second one reaches an agent
  automatically today

- @What it would take@
  - An alerting rule that opens a GitHub issue from a production error, with the
    stack trace, the request that triggered it, and the deployed version attached
  - The issue routes into the same `PR_for_authors` labeling scheme already used for
    human-filed work, so an agent picks it up the same way

- @Key idea@: "coding while you sleep" only closes the loop if a failure the agent
  caused while asleep finds its way back to an agent, not just to an on-call human
// TODO(gp): Look into Sentry/Rollbar/Datadog error-to-issue integrations and
//   PagerDuty-to-GitHub bridges as the tools that would close this loop

* Canary Analysis and Automated Rollback

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 3

- @Problem@: `docker_rollback_dev_image` covers the dev image, and a human decides to
  run it after noticing something is wrong

- @Definition@: A **canary release** ships a change to a small slice of traffic first
  and compares its error rate and latency against the unchanged majority before
  widening the rollout

- @Fact@: The production service already runs blue-green and canary deploys, with an
  automatic rollback when a health check fails; the gap is narrower than "nothing
  exists"

- @Gap@: A health check only sees "is the process up", not "did the error rate or
  latency get worse"
  - No metric-threshold rollback, only liveness-threshold rollback
  - The dev-image path (`docker_rollback_dev_image`) stays a manual command, with no
    equivalent automatic trigger

- @What it would take@
  - A metric comparison job that runs after each deploy and triggers rollback on a
    threshold, not just on a failed health check
  - The same trigger wired to the dev-image rollback path, so a metric regression
    there recovers as fast as a metric regression in prod

- @Key idea@: Automatic rollback already exists for "is it alive"; the open problem
  is automatic rollback for "is it correct"
// TODO(gp): Look into Flagger and Argo Rollouts for canary automation on
//   Kubernetes, Istio traffic splitting, and Prometheus + Alertmanager for
//   SLO-triggered rollback

# Summary

* Why This Tooling Matters for AI-Assisted Development

// From paper "The Causify AIgentic Development System", Part 4

- @Key idea@: Nothing in this stack was built for agents, and that is the point
  - Discoverability, automation, standardization, and immediate feedback happen to be
    what an agent needs

- @Characteristics@: The properties that make a codebase agent-ready
  - _Self-documenting workflows_: `invoke --list` replaces tribal knowledge
  - _Manifests and contracts_: `AGENTS.md` and `repo_config.yaml` answer questions
    instead of hiding answers in prose
  - _Context-bounded editing_: The runnable directory limits what must be read
  - _Guardrails and provenance_: Hooks, linters, and scanners make failures
    detectable, and evidence makes changes reviewable
  - _Deterministic verification_: Golden files and categorized tests turn correctness
    into a diff

- @Fact@: The questions removed are the ones agents answer badly
  - _"How should I format this?"_, _"Where do tests live?"_, _"How do I run them?"_

- @Remark@: The loop is self-reinforcing, since better infrastructure enables better
  assistance, which pays for more infrastructure
// TODO(gp): Look into agent-readiness scoring frameworks and benchmarks
//   (SWE-bench, METR evals) to quantify how agent-friendly a stack actually is

* Tooling Stack: Summary and Open Questions

// From book_AI_Software_Engineering/resources.md
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Lifecycle stage", "Artifact the agent uses"),
  rows: (
    ("Plan the work", "specs.write, ADRs (gap)"),
    ("Understand the repo", "AGENTS.md, rules files, repo_config.yaml"),
    ("Do a task", "Skill files, invoke tasks"),
    ("Change code", "Runnable directory boundary, linter"),
    ("Prove the change", "Fast/slow tests, golden files, coverage, mutation (gap)"),
    ("Deliver", "PR split plan, ai_review.py, CI gates"),
    ("Release", "Docker stages, changelog.txt, registries"),
    ("Learn from production", "Incident-to-issue, canary/rollback (gap)"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.7fr),
)
```

- @Question@: How much autonomy should each gate allow before a human signs off?

- @Question@: When the linter and the model disagree about style, which one is the
  specification?

- @Question@: Rules files consume context, so how large can they grow before they
  cost more than they save?

- @Question@: Who owns a regression introduced by an agent-authored pull request that
  a human approved?

- @Key idea@: The tooling does not make the agent correct, it makes the agent
  _checkable_, which is the property a team can actually build on
